<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lucius Mummius Achaicus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Lucius Mummius</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lucius Mummius Achaicus was a Roman consul and general who defeated the Achaean League in 146 BCE. After his victory he destroyed Corinth, killing many of its men, enslaving women and children and shipping its art treasures to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The league was dissolved and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> came under Roman control, later becoming the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>. Corinth was refounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> in 44 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lucius-mummius-achaicus.jpg" alt="Lucius Mummius Achaicus" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Netelo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:History_of_Ancient_painting_in_Hermitage_-_66_(LXVI)_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lucius Mummius was the first of his family to reach the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a>, which made him a novus homo, a new man. His father had been plebeian tribune in 187 BCE and praetor in 177 BCE. Almost nothing is known of Mummius's early career. He was praetor in 153 BCE and was assigned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania-ulterior">Hispania Ulterior</a>, where his predecessor had been defeated by the Lusitanians. He won successes against them, defeated raiders again in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 151 BCE to celebrate a triumph. After at least one failed attempt, he was elected consul for 146 BCE with Gnaeus Cornelius Lentulus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As consul Mummius took command of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaean-war">Achaean War</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-caecilius-metellus-macedonicus">Metellus Macedonicus</a>, who had already beaten the Achaeans at Scarpheia. He hurried to the camp, ordered Metellus back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-roman-province">Macedonia</a> and routed the Achaeans, who had mobilised all their men and even freed their slaves, at the Isthmus. Corinth's population quickly fled, and Mummius found the city undefended with its gates open. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> sacked it on the senate's instructions. A senatorial commission arrived with orders that Corinth be razed, and the leagues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> were dissolved and the region made a Roman province. Whoever remained in Corinth was sold into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, and its land became Roman public land.</p><p>Mummius gave Corinthian booty and statues to at least seventeen communities and made offerings at Delphi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympia</a>. He was awarded a second triumph and took the name Achaicus, the first new man to receive such a victory name. A remark attributed to him, that those who shipped the treasures of Corinth would have to replace anything lost or damaged, is cited as showing his indifference to art.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As censor in 142 BCE with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus">Scipio Aemilianus</a> he was more lenient than his colleague, and he built a temple to Hercules from the proceeds of the war. He probably died soon after, since by 140 BCE he is spoken of in the past tense. Centuries later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> and Pliny attributed the start of serious philhellenism at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to his triumph. His great-granddaughter Mummia Achaica was the mother of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galba">Galba</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lucius Mummius Achaicus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army's final campaign, in 146 BCE, ended in disaster: Critolaus was defeated by Quintus Caecilius Metellus at Scarpheia in Locris, and Diaeus by <b>Lucius Mummius</b> on the Isthmus, after which Corinth was sacked.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Lucius Mummius</b> destroyed Corinth in 146 BCE; Julius Caesar refounded it as a Roman colony in 44 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/corinth">Corinth</a></li></ul></section>
